# Introduction # {#intro}

Sensor observations, positioning data, measurements, mobility information... are commonly published as time series: a timestamp with one or more values.
Representing time series in RDF drastically raises the verbosity, as each reading is often given its own identifier, for which contextual information is repeated on all readings.
With RDF Time Series Sources, a publisher keeps the readings compact and describes them with RDF:

- A [=Source=] describes a table of readings: every [=Row=] has one time and values for one or more [=Fields=]. Series that are measured together share one [=Time Field=]. Every value of a [=Value Field=] is one reading: a [=Point=].
- The class of a source says where its readings are: embedded in the metadata, as RDF points (`tss:EmbeddedRDFSource`) or as one JSON literal (`tss:EmbeddedJSONSource`), or kept in an external Parquet, CSV or Arrow data file (`tss:ParquetSource`, `tss:CSVSource`, `tss:ArrowSource`). The same field descriptions read them all.
- Each field states the datatype of its values and, for a data file or JSON rows, its column name. Optionally, a value field states what it observes, in which unit, with which sensor, of which feature of interest.
- In RDF, every [=Point=] states its value field, its time and its reading with `tss:field`, `tss:time` and `tss:value`: what its reading means is stated once, on its field. A [=Describing Field=], such as a status flag, states its values on the points with a [=Field Property=] of its own, which by default is made from its column name, as in CSVW [[TABULAR-METADATA]].
- A data file can give the time of each row as a step on a regular [=Time Grid=], and state the values of a field as differences from a baseline (`tss:valueEncoding`).

The metadata also states the [=Time Window=] of the readings, whether readings may still be added, and an optional [=Stored Summary=] of each field. A consumer can follow a source to which readings are still added. When the publisher enables it, the consumer only fetches the new readings, with HTTP Range requests ([[#following]]). Streamed as RDF messages, the readings follow the RDF Message Stream Profile of this specification ([[#message-profile]]).

Every source can be expanded into its points as RDF, in the same shape as the points of a `tss:EmbeddedRDFSource` ([[#expanding]]). This specification does not map the points onto another vocabulary: the TSS vocabulary is aligned with SOSA [[VOCAB-SSN]] and SAREF [[SAREF]], so that a reasoner can derive their observations and measurements from the points when they are needed ([[#alignment]]).

A source usually holds a whole time series, also when it grows large: it is not split into smaller parts for consumers to find. A consumer that only needs part of the time extent can still fetch less, with HTTP Range requests on the formats that allow it. The footer of a Parquet file says where each row group is and, usually, the smallest and largest value of each column in it, so a consumer can fetch the footer first and then only the row groups it needs. The footer of an Arrow file in the IPC file format likewise says where each record batch is.

## Namespaces ## {#namespaces}

RDF Time Series Sources use the following prefix and namespace:

```turtle
@prefix tss: <https://w3id.org/tss#> .
```

Note: Earlier drafts of this specification were called RDF Time Series Snippets. The namespace stays the same, but `tss:Source` was `tss:Snippet`, the five source classes, such as `tss:EmbeddedRDFSource`, ended in `Snippet`, such as `tss:EmbeddedRDFSnippet`, `tss:sourceDescription` was `tss:snippetDescription`, and `tss:ContextField` was `tss:MetaField`. In earlier drafts, a point also stated its time and its reading with a property of the time field and of its value field (`tss:property`), which by default was made from the column name. A point now always states them with `tss:time` and `tss:value`, and only a describing field has a property of its own.

## Embedded example ## {#embedded-example}

This example is a complete [=Embedded Source=]: five conductivity readings of one sensor, embedded in the metadata as RDF points. Readings may still be added to it: it does not state `tss:immutable true`. It is therefore written as an RDF Message Log [[RDF-MESSAGES]]: the first message describes the source and its fields, and each next message holds one row. A writer adds a row by appending one message ([[#updating]]). The source states `tss:rangeRequests true`: its publisher promises that the document only grows, byte for byte, so a consumer that follows the source only fetches the messages it has not read yet ([[#following]]).

<div class="example" id="example-embedded-source">
```turtle
VERSION "1.2-messages"
PREFIX tss: <https://w3id.org/tss#>
PREFIX rdf: <http://www.w3.org/1999/02/22-rdf-syntax-ns#>
PREFIX rdfs: <http://www.w3.org/2000/01/rdf-schema#>
PREFIX xsd: <http://www.w3.org/2001/XMLSchema#>
PREFIX quantitykind: <http://qudt.org/vocab/quantitykind/>
PREFIX unit: <http://qudt.org/vocab/unit/>
PREFIX ex: <https://example.org/ex#>

ex:source
    a tss:EmbeddedRDFSource ;
    rdfs:label "Conductivity readings"@en ;
    tss:rangeRequests true ;
    tss:producedBy ex:producer ;
    tss:from "2024-05-12T08:00:00Z"^^xsd:dateTime ;
    tss:until "2024-05-13T00:00:00Z"^^xsd:dateTime ;
    tss:timeField ex:time-field ;
    tss:key ex:time-field ;
    tss:hasField ex:time-field,
        ex:value-field .

ex:time-field
    a tss:ContextField ;
    rdfs:label "Observation time"@en ;
    tss:datatype xsd:dateTime .

ex:value-field
    a tss:ValueField ;
    rdfs:label "Electrical conductivity"@en ;
    tss:datatype xsd:double ;
    tss:minimum "0"^^xsd:double ;
    tss:maximum "10000"^^xsd:double ;
    tss:observedProperty quantitykind:ElectricConductivity ;
    tss:unit unit:MicroS-PER-CentiM ;
    tss:sensor ex:sensor-1 ;
    tss:featureOfInterest ex:feature-1 .
MESSAGE
ex:source tss:hasPoint [
    tss:time "2024-05-12T08:00:00Z"^^xsd:dateTime ;
    tss:value "4810.5"^^xsd:double
] .
MESSAGE
ex:source tss:hasPoint [
    tss:time "2024-05-12T08:15:00Z"^^xsd:dateTime ;
    tss:value "4722.4"^^xsd:double
] .
MESSAGE
ex:source tss:hasPoint [
    tss:time "2024-05-12T08:30:00Z"^^xsd:dateTime ;
    tss:value "4751.9"^^xsd:double
] .
MESSAGE
ex:source tss:hasPoint [
    tss:time "2024-05-12T09:15:00Z"^^xsd:dateTime ;
    tss:value "4719.3"^^xsd:double
] .
MESSAGE
ex:source tss:hasPoint [
    tss:time "2024-05-12T09:45:00Z"^^xsd:dateTime ;
    tss:value "4783.6"^^xsd:double
] .
MESSAGE
```
</div>

The source is a `tss:EmbeddedRDFSource`: it links to each point with `tss:hasPoint`. Each message holds one row, which here is one point, as the source has one value field. A point states its time with `tss:time` and its reading with `tss:value` ([[#point-values]]). It leaves out `tss:field`, the link to its value field: the source has one value field, so every point is a point of that field. Every message ends with the delimiter `MESSAGE`, so a consumer knows that the last message it read is complete.

Expanded ([[#expanding]]), every point also links to its field. The first point, expanded:

<div class="example" id="example-expanded-point">
```turtle
ex:source tss:hasPoint [
    tss:field ex:value-field ;
    tss:time "2024-05-12T08:00:00Z"^^xsd:dateTime ;
    tss:value "4810.5"^^xsd:double
] .

# ... and four more points
```
</div>

With the alignments of the TSS vocabulary ([[#alignment]]), a reasoner derives from this point a SOSA observation: the point is a `sosa:Observation` whose `sosa:hasSimpleResult` is `4810.5`, whose `sosa:resultTime` is 08:00, and whose observed property, sensor, feature of interest and unit are those of its field.

The examples that follow reuse the prefixes of these examples.

# Definitions # {#definitions}

A <dfn>Time Series</dfn> is a set of data points ordered by time, where each data point consists of a timestamp and a value.

A <dfn>Source</dfn> holds the readings of one or more time series that share one time field, and is described by one node of one of the five source classes ([[#storage]]). It is a table: each row is a [=Row=], each column a [=Field=]. Each [=Value Field=] holds one [=Time Series=].

A <dfn>Field</dfn> is one column of a [=Source=], described by one `tss:Field`: the datatype of its values, where they are, and, optionally, what they mean. A field is a [=Value Field=] or a [=Context Field=] ([[#field-kinds]]).

A <dfn>Value Field</dfn> (`tss:ValueField`) is a field whose values are readings: each of its values is a [=Point=].

A <dfn>Context Field</dfn> (`tss:ContextField`) is a field whose values are not readings, but give the context of a row or of the points in it: the [=Time Field=], the [=Step Field=], a [=Describing Field=], or another field such as an identifier.

A <dfn>Row</dfn> is one row of a [=Source=]: one time, and at most one value for each [=Field=].

A <dfn>Point</dfn> is one reading (`tss:Point`): the value of one [=Value Field=] in one [=Row=], with the time of that row and the values of the describing fields that describe it.

The <dfn>Field Property</dfn> of a [=Describing Field=] is the RDF property that states its values on the points: its `tss:property`, or, when it states none, its default property ([[#point-values]]).

The <dfn>Time Field</dfn> of a source is the field that holds the time of each row (`tss:timeField`).

The <dfn>Key</dfn> of a source is the field whose values identify a row: its [=Time Field=] (`tss:key`).

The <dfn>Time Window</dfn> of a source is the period from `tss:from` (included) until `tss:until` (not included). A row, and every point in it, lies in the window when its time is not before `tss:from` and before `tss:until`. A bound that is not stated leaves that side of the window open.

An <dfn>Embedded Source</dfn> keeps its rows in the metadata: a `tss:EmbeddedRDFSource` as points, RDF nodes that it links to with `tss:hasPoint`, a `tss:EmbeddedJSONSource` as one JSON literal, `tss:points`. An <dfn>External Source</dfn> keeps them in a data file, `tss:dataFile`: a `tss:ParquetSource`, a `tss:CSVSource` or a `tss:ArrowSource`.

The <dfn id="dfn-time-grid">Time Grid</dfn> of an external source is a series of times at regular steps: step 0 at `tss:timeBase`, and every next step `tss:timeStep` later ([[#time-grid]]).

The <dfn>Step Field</dfn> of an external source with a time grid is the field whose column holds the step of each row (`tss:stepField`). Without one, the step of a row is its position in the data file.

An <dfn>Empty Value</dfn> is the absence of a value: an empty CSV cell, a Parquet or Arrow null, a row of RDF points without a point for a value field, an RDF point without a triple for a describing field, a JSON row without the field's key or with `null` for it, or the empty text `""`. A text that holds only spaces is not empty. Consequently, the empty string cannot be a value: an empty text and no value are the same thing.

Note: This is a known limitation: a field cannot hold the empty string as a value. A property that states which text means "no value" could lift it.

The <dfn>Decoded Value</dfn> of a value is what the value stands for: the value itself, or, for a field with a `tss:valueEncoding`, the value decoded as its encoding says ([[#value-encoding]]).

A <dfn>Describing Field</dfn> is a [=Context Field=] that says something about the values of a value field in the same row (`tss:describesField`).

A <dfn>Stored Summary</dfn> holds numbers computed from the values of one field, such as their count and mean (`tss:summary`).

A <dfn>Growing Source</dfn> is a source that does not state `tss:immutable true`: rows may still be added to it ([[#change]]).

A <dfn>Write</dfn> is a change to the rows of a source: adding a row, since rows and points are never changed or removed ([[#change]]).

# The source # {#the-source}

A metadata document MUST describe at least one source. Each source in the document is described and read on its own: it has its own fields, rows and data file, and each field, each row and each point belongs to exactly one source. A source has one time field, which gives the time of every row for all its other fields. The table lists the properties of a source. "Required" means exactly one value unless the table says otherwise, "optional" at most one, and "not allowed" none.

<table class="data">
<thead>
<tr><th>Property</th><th>Value</th><th>External source</th><th>Embedded source</th></tr>
</thead>
<tbody>
<tr><td><code>tss:immutable</code></td><td><code>xsd:boolean</code></td><td>optional; default <code>false</code></td><td>optional; default <code>false</code></td></tr>
<tr><td><code>tss:rangeRequests</code></td><td><code>xsd:boolean</code></td><td>optional; default <code>false</code>; <code>true</code> only for a <code>tss:CSVSource</code>, or a <code>tss:ArrowSource</code> in the IPC streaming format</td><td>optional; default <code>false</code>; <code>true</code> only for a <code>tss:EmbeddedRDFSource</code></td></tr>
<tr><td><code>tss:hasField</code></td><td>a <code>tss:Field</code></td><td>required, one or more</td><td>required, one or more</td></tr>
<tr><td><code>tss:timeField</code></td><td>one of its fields</td><td>required</td><td>required</td></tr>
<tr><td><code>tss:key</code></td><td>its time field</td><td>optional; default: the time field</td><td>optional; default: the time field</td></tr>
<tr><td><code>tss:from</code></td><td>a time ([[#times]])</td><td>optional</td><td>optional</td></tr>
<tr><td><code>tss:until</code></td><td>a time ([[#times]]), after <code>tss:from</code></td><td>optional</td><td>optional</td></tr>
<tr><td><code>tss:hasPoint</code></td><td>a point</td><td>not allowed</td><td>for a <code>tss:EmbeddedRDFSource</code>: optional, any number; otherwise not allowed</td></tr>
<tr><td><code>tss:points</code></td><td><code>rdf:JSON</code> literal</td><td>not allowed</td><td>for a <code>tss:EmbeddedJSONSource</code>: required; otherwise not allowed</td></tr>
<tr><td><code>tss:dataFile</code></td><td>IRI</td><td>required</td><td>not allowed</td></tr>
<tr><td><code>tss:delimiter</code></td><td>one character</td><td>optional, for a <code>tss:CSVSource</code>; default <code>","</code></td><td>not allowed</td></tr>
<tr><td><code>tss:encoding</code></td><td>text, such as <code>"utf-8"</code></td><td>optional, for a <code>tss:CSVSource</code>; default <code>"utf-8"</code></td><td>not allowed</td></tr>
<tr><td><code>tss:header</code></td><td><code>xsd:boolean</code></td><td>optional, for a <code>tss:CSVSource</code>; default <code>true</code></td><td>not allowed</td></tr>
<tr><td><code>tss:timeBase</code></td><td>a time ([[#times]])</td><td>optional, together with <code>tss:timeStep</code></td><td>not allowed</td></tr>
<tr><td><code>tss:timeStep</code></td><td><code>xsd:duration</code>, more than zero</td><td>optional, together with <code>tss:timeBase</code></td><td>not allowed</td></tr>
<tr><td><code>tss:stepField</code></td><td>one of its fields</td><td>optional with a time grid; not allowed without one</td><td>not allowed</td></tr>
<tr><td><code>tss:producedBy</code></td><td>IRI</td><td>optional</td><td>optional</td></tr>
<tr><td><code>rdfs:label</code></td><td>literal</td><td>optional, any number</td><td>optional, any number</td></tr>
<tr><td><code>tss:sourceDescription</code></td><td>a node, any vocabulary</td><td>optional</td><td>optional</td></tr>
</tbody>
</table>

## Where the readings are ## {#storage}

The class of a source says where its rows are and how to read them. `tss:Source` has five subclasses, the source classes:

<table class="data">
<thead>
<tr><th>Class</th><th>Where the rows are</th><th>Section</th></tr>
</thead>
<tbody>
<tr><td><code>tss:EmbeddedJSONSource</code></td><td>in the metadata, as one JSON literal, <code>tss:points</code>, with one JSON object per row</td><td>[[#json-points]]</td></tr>
<tr><td><code>tss:EmbeddedRDFSource</code></td><td>in the metadata, as RDF points that the source links to with <code>tss:hasPoint</code></td><td>[[#rdf-points]]</td></tr>
<tr><td><code>tss:ParquetSource</code></td><td>in a Parquet data file, <code>tss:dataFile</code></td><td>[[#parquet]]</td></tr>
<tr><td><code>tss:CSVSource</code></td><td>in a CSV data file, <code>tss:dataFile</code></td><td>[[#csv]]</td></tr>
<tr><td><code>tss:ArrowSource</code></td><td>in an Arrow data file, <code>tss:dataFile</code></td><td>[[#arrow]]</td></tr>
</tbody>
</table>

Every source MUST state exactly one source class as its `rdf:type`. A consumer selects the rules for a source by this one class: a source without a source class does not say where its readings are.

- An [=Embedded Source=] keeps its rows in the metadata itself ([[#points]]). Every row states its own time and its readings themselves. The source MUST NOT state `tss:dataFile`, `tss:delimiter`, `tss:encoding` or `tss:header`, which describe a data file, nor `tss:timeBase`, `tss:timeStep` or `tss:stepField`, which describe a [=Time Grid=] ([[#time-grid]]). Its fields MUST NOT state a value encoding ([[#value-encoding]]). A `tss:EmbeddedJSONSource` has no `tss:hasPoint`, and a `tss:EmbeddedRDFSource` has no `tss:points`.
- An [=External Source=] keeps its rows in the data file `tss:dataFile`, read as its class says ([[#data-files]]). It has no `tss:hasPoint` and no `tss:points`.

All sources of a document MUST be embedded sources, or all MUST be external sources: a document never describes both.

## Time ## {#time}

### Times ### {#times}

A time of a source is a date and a time of day with a timezone, such as `"2024-05-12T08:00:00Z"`: an `xsd:dateTime` [[XMLSCHEMA11-2]] whose timezone is not left out. The times of a source are:

- on the source: `tss:from`, `tss:until` and `tss:timeBase`;
- on a [=Stored Summary=]: `tss:firstTime`, `tss:lastTime` and `tss:generatedAtTime`;
- in each row: its value for the [=Time Field=], which is also the time of every point in the row.

The `tss:datatype` of the time field MUST be `xsd:dateTime` or `xsd:dateTimeStamp`. A time stated in the metadata MUST be a literal with one of these two datatypes. Every time MUST have a timezone: `Z` or an offset such as `+02:00`; a time without one is not valid, also when its datatype is `xsd:dateTime`, which allows one to be left out elsewhere. A publisher SHOULD use `xsd:dateTime`: `xsd:dateTimeStamp` says the same, as an `xsd:dateTime` whose timezone cannot be left out, but fewer RDF systems support it. Times are compared as the moments they stand for: two times that are the same moment are the same time.

### The time field and the key ### {#time-and-key}

`tss:timeField` names the [=Time Field=]. It MUST be one of the source's own fields (`tss:hasField`). Every row MUST have a time: its value for the time field. In a data file with a [=Time Grid=], the time of a row is the time of its step ([[#time-grid]]).

`tss:key` names the field whose values identify a row: the [=Key=]. The key of a source is always its time field: a source states at most one `tss:key`, and when it states one, it MUST be its time field (`tss:timeField`). When a source does not state `tss:key`, its key is still its time field. No two rows may have the same time; two times that are the same moment are the same time. A point is identified by its row and its value field: no two points of the same value field have the same time.

Note: The key has one field because a [=Field=] identifies its series: the sensor, the feature of interest and the observed property are stated once, on the field, not in a column of the rows. A second key field, such as a station, would put several series in one field, and their points and summaries could not be told apart. Several stations at the same time are written as one field per station, or as one source per station.

### The time window ### {#window}

`tss:from` and `tss:until` state the [=Time Window=] of the source: the readings lie in [`tss:from`, `tss:until`), so a row at exactly `tss:until` is not in the window. Both are times of the source ([[#times]]), and `tss:until` MUST come after `tss:from`. The time of every row of an embedded source MUST lie in the window. Consumers of an external source use only the rows inside the window: the data file may hold more.

Note: The window does not have to be tight: `tss:until` may lie after the newest row, as in <a href="#example-embedded-source">the embedded example</a>. The window of a [=Growing Source=] does not change ([[#change]]), so it is set to cover the readings that will still be added, such as a whole day, or left open.

## Changes ## {#change}

`tss:immutable` says whether readings may still be added to the source, as `ldes:immutable` does for a page of a Linked Data Event Stream:

- `true` means the source is final: no row or point is added, changed or removed, and nothing in its description changes any more.
- `false`, or no `tss:immutable`, means the source is a [=Growing Source=]: it is append-only. Rows may be added ([[#updating]]), but no row or point is changed or removed.

A source that states `tss:immutable true` MUST NOT become a growing source again. A writer ends a growing source by adding `tss:immutable true` ([[#ending]]).

While a source is growing, its description does not change: a [=Write=] only adds rows. Its class, its fields, its [=Time Window=], its data file and its [=Time Grid=] MUST stay as they are. The only statements that are added to its description are `tss:immutable true` and the [=Stored Summaries=] of its fields, at the moment the writer ends the source ([[#ending]]). Every row that is added MUST lie in the time window: a writer that has readings outside of it starts a new source.

A consumer learns that rows were added by fetching the source again. By default, it reads the whole document or data file again. When the source states `tss:rangeRequests true`, it fetches only the new bytes, with HTTP Range requests ([[#range-opt-in]], [[#following]]).

Note: Earlier drafts stated `tss:mutable` and `tss:committedUntil`. `tss:mutable true` is now the default, `tss:mutable false` is `tss:immutable true`, and a consumer finds out whether a growing source changed with HTTP validators (`ETag`, `Last-Modified`) instead of `tss:committedUntil` ([[#following]]).

## Description and provenance ## {#provenance}

- `tss:producedBy` is the agent that produced the data, as one IRI.
- `rdfs:label` names the source for people.
- `tss:sourceDescription` points to a node that describes the source in any vocabulary, for example where it is:

<div class="example" id="example-source-description">
```turtle
@prefix dcterms: <http://purl.org/dc/terms/> .

ex:source
    tss:sourceDescription [
        dcterms:spatial [
            a dcterms:Location ;
            rdfs:label "Site A"@en
        ]
    ] .
```
</div>

# Fields # {#fields}

Each [=Field=] is a `tss:Field`, linked from exactly one source with `tss:hasField`. The same field description reads a column of a data file, a key of the JSON rows and the values of the RDF points. A field is never itself the predicate of its values ([[#point-values]]).

<table class="data">
<thead>
<tr><th>Property</th><th>Value</th><th>Required</th><th>Meaning</th></tr>
</thead>
<tbody>
<tr><td><code>rdf:type</code></td><td><code>tss:ValueField</code> or <code>tss:ContextField</code></td><td>required: exactly one of the two</td><td>whether its values are readings ([[#field-kinds]])</td></tr>
<tr><td><code>tss:columnName</code></td><td>non-empty text</td><td>required for a field of an external source, except the time field of a source with a [=Time Grid=], which has none; required for a field of a <code>tss:EmbeddedJSONSource</code>; for a field of a <code>tss:EmbeddedRDFSource</code>, required for a describing field that states no <code>tss:property</code>, otherwise optional</td><td>the name of the field's column (the CSV header, the Parquet or Arrow column), or its key in the JSON rows; unique within the source</td></tr>
<tr><td><code>tss:property</code></td><td>IRI of an <code>rdf:Property</code></td><td>optional, for a describing field only; default: see [[#point-values]]</td><td>the property that states the field's values on the points it describes; unique within the source</td></tr>
<tr><td><code>tss:datatype</code></td><td>IRI</td><td>required</td><td>the datatype of the values, such as <code>xsd:double</code></td></tr>
<tr><td><code>tss:columnIndex</code></td><td><code>xsd:integer</code>, 0 or more</td><td>optional; none for the time field of a source with a [=Time Grid=]</td><td>the position of the column, the first being 0; unique within the source</td></tr>
<tr><td><code>tss:language</code></td><td>language tag</td><td>optional</td><td>the language of the values of an <code>rdf:langString</code> field</td></tr>
<tr><td><code>tss:minimum</code>, <code>tss:maximum</code></td><td>number</td><td>optional</td><td>the expected range of the [=Decoded Values=], bounds included; the maximum is not below the minimum. Informative: see [[#meaning]]</td></tr>
<tr><td><code>tss:valueEncoding</code></td><td>IRI, such as <code>tss:DeltaFromBaseline</code></td><td>optional, for a field of an external source</td><td>how the values in the data file are stated when they are not the readings themselves ([[#value-encoding]])</td></tr>
<tr><td><code>tss:baseline</code></td><td>number</td><td>with <code>tss:DeltaFromBaseline</code> only</td><td>the number that each value is the difference from ([[#value-encoding]])</td></tr>
<tr><td><code>tss:observedProperty</code></td><td>IRI</td><td>optional, for a value field</td><td>what the values observe</td></tr>
<tr><td><code>tss:unit</code></td><td>IRI</td><td>optional</td><td>the unit of the values</td></tr>
<tr><td><code>tss:sensor</code></td><td>IRI</td><td>optional, for a value field</td><td>the sensor that made the values</td></tr>
<tr><td><code>tss:featureOfInterest</code></td><td>IRI</td><td>optional, for a value field</td><td>the thing the values are about</td></tr>
<tr><td><code>tss:aboutUrl</code></td><td>URI template, as text</td><td>optional, for a value field of a source that is not a <code>tss:EmbeddedRDFSource</code></td><td>how the IRI of each point of the field is made ([[#point-iris]])</td></tr>
<tr><td><code>tss:describesField</code></td><td>a value field of the same source</td><td>optional, any number, for a context field</td><td>the field whose values this field says something about ([[#describing]])</td></tr>
<tr><td><code>tss:summary</code></td><td>a node</td><td>optional</td><td>a [=Stored Summary=] of the values ([[#summaries]])</td></tr>
<tr><td><code>rdfs:label</code>, <code>rdfs:comment</code></td><td>literal</td><td>optional, any number</td><td>for people</td></tr>
<tr><td><code>tss:fieldDescription</code></td><td>a node, any vocabulary</td><td>optional</td><td>a free description of the field</td></tr>
</tbody>
</table>

## Value fields and context fields ## {#field-kinds}

Every field MUST state exactly one of `tss:ValueField` and `tss:ContextField` as its `rdf:type`. Both are subclasses of `tss:Field`.

- A [=Value Field=] holds readings: each of its values is a [=Point=] ([[#points]]). A value field may state what its values observe ([[#meaning]]).
- A [=Context Field=] holds values that are not readings. The time field, the step field and every describing field MUST be context fields. Any other field whose values are not readings, such as an identifier that the IRIs of the points are made from ([[#point-iris]]), is a context field too. A context field MUST NOT state `tss:observedProperty`, `tss:sensor`, `tss:featureOfInterest` or `tss:aboutUrl`.

A source SHOULD have at least one value field: a source without one holds no readings. The values of a context field only end up in a point in two ways: the value of the time field is the time of every point in its row, and the value of a describing field is stated on the points that it describes ([[#describing]]). The values of the other context fields, such as the step field, are not stated on the points.

## How points state values ## {#point-values}

In RDF, every [=Point=] states its value field, its time and its reading with the same three properties of the TSS vocabulary: in the points of a `tss:EmbeddedRDFSource` ([[#rdf-points]]), and in the points that a source of any class is expanded into ([[#expanding]]).

- `tss:field` states its value field;
- `tss:time` states the time of its row: the value of the time field;
- `tss:value` states its reading: the value of its value field.

A field is never itself the predicate of its values, and the predicate does not say what a reading means: the value field of the point says that ([[#meaning]]). Through `tss:time` and `tss:value`, every point is aligned with SOSA and SAREF ([[#alignment]]).

The value of a [=Describing Field=] is stated on the points that it describes with the [=Field Property=] of the describing field ([[#describing]]). A describing field states it with `tss:property`, the IRI of an `rdf:Property`, as a column in CSVW states it with `csvw:propertyUrl` [[TABULAR-METADATA]]. A describing field that states no `tss:property` has a default property: the IRI made of a base IRI, `#`, and the field's `tss:columnName` percent-encoded as a fragment [[RFC3986]], as the default `{#_name}` of `csvw:propertyUrl` is made. The base IRI is the IRI of the data file for an external source, and the IRI of the metadata document for an embedded source.

For example, the describing field with column name `status` of a source whose data file is `https://example.org/data.csv` has the default property `https://example.org/data.csv#status`. A describing field of a `tss:EmbeddedRDFSource` has no default property unless it states a `tss:columnName`, so it MUST state `tss:property` or `tss:columnName`.

- Only a describing field states `tss:property`: the time field, the value fields and the other context fields MUST NOT state one.
- A field property MUST NOT be `rdf:type` or a term of the TSS vocabulary, such as `tss:field`, `tss:time`, `tss:value` or `tss:hasPoint`. Two describing fields of the same source MUST NOT have the same field property.
- A property that a describing field states keeps the meaning that its own vocabulary gives it, and a default property is a property of its own. This specification does not align either: the value of a describing field says something about a reading, and is not a reading itself.

## Point IRIs ## {#point-iris}

A point is a blank node, unless its value field states with `tss:aboutUrl` how the IRI of each of its points is made, as `csvw:aboutUrl` does for the cells of a column [[TABULAR-METADATA]]. The value of `tss:aboutUrl` is a URI template [[RFC6570]]. For each row with a value for the field, the template is expanded, and the result resolved against the base IRI of [[#point-values]], into the IRI of the point:

- Each variable of the template is the `tss:columnName` of a field of the source, and stands for the value of that field in the row, as it is written. An [=Empty Value=] leaves the variable undefined.
- Two points of a source MUST NOT get the same IRI: the template usually holds the time, or another value that differs from row to row, and a part of its own for each value field.

The points of a `tss:EmbeddedRDFSource` are RDF nodes already, IRIs or blank nodes, so its fields do not state `tss:aboutUrl`.

<div class="example" id="example-about-url">
A value field whose points get an IRI from the time of their row:

```turtle
ex:csv-value-field
    tss:aboutUrl "https://example.org/obs/value/{time}" .
```

The point of the row with time `2024-05-01T00:00:00Z` is `https://example.org/obs/value/2024-05-01T00%3A00%3A00Z`: a simple expansion percent-encodes the colons.
</div>

## Values and datatypes ## {#datatypes}

Every value is a text: a CSV cell, a JSON string of a JSON row, the lexical form of a literal in an RDF point, or a Parquet or Arrow value written in the lexical form of its type ([[#parquet]], [[#arrow]]). `tss:datatype` is the logical datatype that reads that text; the data file keeps its own types. In an RDF point, every value MUST be a literal whose datatype is the field's `tss:datatype`. A value MUST be in the lexical form of its datatype [[XMLSCHEMA11-2]]; an empty text is no value ([=Empty Value=]), so it is not checked. Implementations check at least these forms:

- `xsd:double`, `xsd:float` and `xsd:decimal`; `xsd:integer`, `xsd:long`, `xsd:int`, `xsd:short`, `xsd:byte`, `xsd:nonNegativeInteger`, `xsd:positiveInteger`, `xsd:nonPositiveInteger`, `xsd:negativeInteger` and the unsigned types;
- `xsd:boolean` (`true`, `false`, `1`, `0`);
- `xsd:dateTime`, `xsd:dateTimeStamp` and `xsd:date`, which must also be real days: 30 February is no date.

Any other datatype, such as `xsd:string`, accepts any text. A value keeps its text exactly as it is written: `" 12 "` is not read as 12, so a check can report it. The numeric datatypes are `xsd:double`, `xsd:float`, `xsd:decimal` and the XSD integer types: only a numeric field has an observed minimum and maximum, a mean and a median in its [=Stored Summary=].

An `rdf:langString` field states the language of its values with `tss:language`.

## What a field observes ## {#meaning}

`tss:observedProperty`, `tss:unit`, `tss:sensor` and `tss:featureOfInterest` say what the values of a value field mean. They are stated field by field, so the fields of one source can observe different properties, with different sensors, in different units. They are not repeated on the points: a point links to its field with `tss:field` ([[#point-values]]), and a reasoner derives the observed property, sensor, feature of interest and unit of the point from its field ([[#alignment]]).

`tss:minimum` and `tss:maximum` are the expected range of a numeric field, bounds included, and each of them applies on its own. They are not the range of the values that occur: those are in the stored summary (`tss:observedMinimum`, `tss:observedMaximum`). The range is informative: a value outside it does not make the source non-conforming, and the expansion keeps it. A consumer MAY use the range, for example to flag suspicious readings.

<div class="example" id="example-multi-sensor">
Several value fields share one time field, each with its own observed property, unit and sensor. Each point states its value field with `tss:field`. The rows at 08:00 and 08:20 each have two points, one for each value field; the row at 08:10 has no humidity, so it only has a point for the temperature. The source states no `tss:key`, so its key is its time field.

```turtle
ex:weather-source
    a tss:EmbeddedRDFSource ;
    rdfs:label "Weather station readings"@en ;
    tss:immutable true ;
    tss:from "2024-05-12T08:00:00Z"^^xsd:dateTime ;
    tss:until "2024-05-12T09:00:00Z"^^xsd:dateTime ;
    tss:timeField ex:weather-time-field ;
    tss:hasField ex:weather-time-field,
        ex:temperature-field,
        ex:humidity-field .

ex:weather-time-field
    a tss:ContextField ;
    tss:datatype xsd:dateTime .

ex:temperature-field
    a tss:ValueField ;
    tss:datatype xsd:double ;
    tss:observedProperty quantitykind:Temperature ;
    tss:unit unit:DEG_C ;
    tss:sensor ex:thermometer ;
    tss:featureOfInterest ex:outside-air .

ex:humidity-field
    a tss:ValueField ;
    tss:datatype xsd:double ;
    tss:minimum "0"^^xsd:double ;
    tss:maximum "100"^^xsd:double ;
    tss:observedProperty quantitykind:RelativeHumidity ;
    tss:unit unit:PERCENT_RH ;
    tss:sensor ex:hygrometer ;
    tss:featureOfInterest ex:outside-air .

ex:weather-source tss:hasPoint [
    tss:field ex:temperature-field ;
    tss:time "2024-05-12T08:00:00Z"^^xsd:dateTime ;
    tss:value "18.2"^^xsd:double
], [
    tss:field ex:humidity-field ;
    tss:time "2024-05-12T08:00:00Z"^^xsd:dateTime ;
    tss:value "61.0"^^xsd:double
] .

ex:weather-source tss:hasPoint [
    tss:field ex:temperature-field ;
    tss:time "2024-05-12T08:10:00Z"^^xsd:dateTime ;
    tss:value "18.4"^^xsd:double
] .

ex:weather-source tss:hasPoint [
    tss:field ex:temperature-field ;
    tss:time "2024-05-12T08:20:00Z"^^xsd:dateTime ;
    tss:value "18.9"^^xsd:double
], [
    tss:field ex:humidity-field ;
    tss:time "2024-05-12T08:20:00Z"^^xsd:dateTime ;
    tss:value "59.5"^^xsd:double
] .
```

The three rows hold five points.
</div>

## Describing fields ## {#describing}

A [=Describing Field=] is a context field that says something about the values of a value field of the same source, in the same row, such as a status or a quality flag. It states that field with `tss:describesField`; it may describe several. Its value is not a point of its own: it is stated, with the describing field's [=Field Property=], on the point of each field it describes in that row. A point of a field that a describing field describes has at most one value for that describing field.

<div class="example" id="example-describing-field">
Two series in one embedded source: a value, and a status that describes it. The status is stated on the point of the value, with the property `ex:status` of the status field. The source has one value field, so its points leave out `tss:field`.

```turtle
ex:two-series-source
    a tss:EmbeddedRDFSource ;
    tss:immutable true ;
    tss:from "2024-05-12T08:00:00Z"^^xsd:dateTime ;
    tss:until "2024-05-13T00:00:00Z"^^xsd:dateTime ;
    tss:timeField ex:two-series-time-field ;
    tss:key ex:two-series-time-field ;
    tss:hasField ex:two-series-time-field,
        ex:two-series-value-field,
        ex:two-series-status-field .

ex:two-series-time-field
    a tss:ContextField ;
    tss:datatype xsd:dateTime .

ex:two-series-value-field
    a tss:ValueField ;
    tss:datatype xsd:double ;
    tss:observedProperty quantitykind:ElectricConductivity ;
    tss:unit unit:MicroS-PER-CentiM .

ex:two-series-status-field
    a tss:ContextField ;
    tss:property ex:status ;
    tss:datatype xsd:string ;
    tss:describesField ex:two-series-value-field .

ex:two-series-source tss:hasPoint [
    tss:time "2024-05-12T08:00:00Z"^^xsd:dateTime ;
    tss:value "4810.5"^^xsd:double ;
    ex:status "ok"^^xsd:string
] .

ex:two-series-source tss:hasPoint [
    tss:time "2024-05-12T08:15:00Z"^^xsd:dateTime ;
    tss:value "4722.4"^^xsd:double ;
    ex:status "estimated"^^xsd:string
] .
```
</div>

`tss:fieldDescription`, like `tss:sourceDescription`, points to a node that describes the field in any vocabulary:

<div class="example" id="example-field-description">
```turtle
ex:value-field
    tss:fieldDescription [
        dcterms:description "Electrical conductivity"@en ;
        dcterms:subject quantitykind:ElectricConductivity ;
        dcterms:relation ex:sensor-1, ex:feature-1
    ] .
```
</div>

# Embedded rows # {#points}

An [=Embedded Source=] keeps its rows in the metadata. Its class says how they are written: as RDF points in a `tss:EmbeddedRDFSource` ([[#rdf-points]]), or as one JSON literal in a `tss:EmbeddedJSONSource` ([[#json-points]]). Either way:

- Every row has a value for the time field ([[#time-and-key]]), and states its readings themselves: an embedded source has no [=Time Grid=] and no value encoding.
- A row has at most one value for each field.
- The rows and points have no order. A consumer orders them by their time.
- A source may have no rows yet.

## RDF points ## {#rdf-points}

A `tss:EmbeddedRDFSource` keeps its readings as RDF points. The source links to each [=Point=] with `tss:hasPoint`, and a point is a point of exactly one source. Every node that a source links to with `tss:hasPoint` is a point: its type follows from that link, so a point states no `rdf:type`. It is usually a blank node, written in one statement with the other points of its row, as in <a href="#example-embedded-source">the embedded example</a>; a publisher MAY give it an IRI. A point states its value field, its time, its reading and the values of the describing fields of its row, each in one triple with the point as subject ([[#point-values]]). Every value is a literal with the `tss:datatype` of its field ([[#datatypes]]):

- A point has exactly one `tss:field` triple, whose object is a value field of its source: the value field of the point. When the source has exactly one value field, a point MAY leave it out: its value field is then that field. The expansion adds it to every point ([[#expanding]]).
- A point has exactly one `tss:time` triple: the time of its row, the value of the time field.
- A point has exactly one `tss:value` triple: its reading, the value of its value field.
- A point has at most one triple with the [=Field Property=] of each describing field that describes its value field ([[#describing]]), and no triple with the field property of any other describing field.
- The points of a source with the same time form one [=Row=]. A row has at most one point for each value field: no two points of a source have the same value field and the same time. A row without a point for a value field has an [=Empty Value=] for it, and a point without a triple for a describing field has an empty value for that field. A value with the empty text `""` is no value either.
- A point SHOULD have no other triples: values of other properties are not values of the source.

What the reading of a point means is stated once, on its value field ([[#meaning]]), and what the value of a describing field means, on that describing field.

The metadata document of a [=Growing Source=] of this class MUST be an RDF Message Log [[RDF-MESSAGES]], such as Turtle or TriG that announces the version label `1.2-messages`. Its first message describes the sources and their fields, and every next message holds one row: the points of one time. A [=Write=] then adds a row by appending one message ([[#updating]]). When the source states `tss:rangeRequests true`, a consumer reads the new rows without reading the document again ([[#following]]). The metadata document is read as the RDF merge of its messages: since blank node identifiers are scoped to the message they occur in, the blank nodes of two rows are never the same node. A writer MUST end every message of the log of a growing source with a message delimiter (`MESSAGE`, or `@message .`), so a consumer can tell a complete message from one that is still being written. A delimiter right before the end of the document does not start a new message [[RDF-MESSAGES]]. The log follows the RDF Message Stream Profile of this specification ([[#message-profile]]).

## JSON rows ## {#json-points}

A `tss:EmbeddedJSONSource` MUST have exactly one `tss:points`: a literal with datatype `rdf:JSON` [[JSON-LD11]] whose value is a JSON array of JSON objects, one object per [=Row=]. Each row holds one [=Point=] for each value field it has a value for. The empty array `[]` holds no rows.

- The keys of an object are the `tss:columnName` of the source's fields, so every field of a `tss:EmbeddedJSONSource` MUST state a `tss:columnName`. A row has no key for a field it has no value for.
- A value SHOULD be a JSON string that holds the lexical form of the field's datatype: `"2.50"`, not `2.50`. A JSON number loses its text when it is read: `2.50` may come back as `2.5` or as `2.5E0`.
- A missing key, `null` and `""` are an [=Empty Value=]: the row has no value for that field.
- A key MUST NOT start with `@`, as JSON-LD keywords such as `@context` and `@id` do.
- Every key SHOULD be the column name of a field. Values under other keys are not values of the source.

No key has a meaning of its own: the fields say what each key means. A row has no identifier; the points in it get an IRI only from `tss:aboutUrl` ([[#point-iris]]).

<div class="example" id="example-json-points">
The readings of <a href="#example-embedded-source">the embedded example</a> as JSON rows:

```turtle
ex:json-source
    a tss:EmbeddedJSONSource ;
    tss:immutable false ;
    tss:from "2024-05-12T08:00:00Z"^^xsd:dateTime ;
    tss:until "2024-05-13T00:00:00Z"^^xsd:dateTime ;
    tss:timeField ex:json-time-field ;
    tss:hasField ex:json-time-field,
        ex:json-value-field ;
    tss:points """[
        { "time": "2024-05-12T08:00:00Z", "value": "4810.5" },
        { "time": "2024-05-12T08:15:00Z", "value": "4722.4" },
        { "time": "2024-05-12T08:30:00Z", "value": "4751.9" },
        { "time": "2024-05-12T09:15:00Z", "value": "4719.3" },
        { "time": "2024-05-12T09:45:00Z", "value": "4783.6" }
      ]"""^^rdf:JSON .

ex:json-time-field
    a tss:ContextField ;
    tss:columnName "time" ;
    tss:datatype xsd:dateTime .

ex:json-value-field
    a tss:ValueField ;
    tss:columnName "value" ;
    tss:datatype xsd:double .
```

The keys of the rows are the fields' `tss:columnName`. The source states no `tss:key`, so its key is its time field. Expanded, each row is one point, which states its reading with `tss:value` ([[#expanding]]).
</div>

# External data files # {#data-files}

An [=External Source=] keeps its rows in the file `tss:dataFile`, an IRI that is resolved against the IRI of the metadata document, so it may be relative (`<data.csv>`). Its class says how to read the file: a `tss:CSVSource` as CSV ([[#csv]]), a `tss:ParquetSource` as Parquet ([[#parquet]]) and a `tss:ArrowSource` as Arrow ([[#arrow]]). The metadata does not change the file: the file keeps its own column names and types, and the fields say how to read them. Each row of the file is a [=Row=], which holds one [=Point=] for each value field it has a value for.

## CSV ## {#csv}

The data file of a `tss:CSVSource` is read as CSV [[RFC4180]]:

- `tss:delimiter` is the one character that separates the cells, such as `","` or `";"`. When it is not stated, it is `","`.
- `tss:encoding` is the text encoding of the file. When it is not stated, it is `"utf-8"`.
- `tss:header` says whether the first line names the columns. When it is not stated, the file has a header line.
- With a header line, a field's column is the column whose name is the field's `tss:columnName`. Without one (`tss:header false`), it is the column at the field's `tss:columnIndex`: the first column is 0.
- An empty cell is an [=Empty Value=].

<div class="example" id="example-csv-source">
An external source with a CSV file. It states its delimiter; the header line and the encoding are the defaults. It states no `tss:key`, so its key is its time field.

```turtle
ex:csv-source
    a tss:CSVSource ;
    tss:immutable true ;
    tss:from "2024-05-01T00:00:00Z"^^xsd:dateTime ;
    tss:dataFile <https://example.org/data.csv> ;
    tss:delimiter ";" ;
    tss:timeField ex:csv-time-field ;
    tss:hasField ex:csv-time-field,
        ex:csv-value-field .

ex:csv-time-field
    a tss:ContextField ;
    tss:columnName "time" ;
    tss:datatype xsd:dateTime .

ex:csv-value-field
    a tss:ValueField ;
    tss:columnName "value" ;
    tss:datatype xsd:double .
```

The first lines of the data file:

```
time;value
2024-05-01T00:00:00Z;12.5
2024-05-01T00:15:00Z;12.7
```
</div>

<div class="example" id="example-csv-no-header">
An external source with a CSV file without a header line, and with an open time window: it states neither `tss:from` nor `tss:until`, so every row of the file is read. The columns are found by their `tss:columnIndex`.

```turtle
ex:headerless-source
    a tss:CSVSource ;
    tss:immutable false ;
    tss:dataFile <data-no-header.csv> ;
    tss:header false ;
    tss:timeField ex:headerless-time-field ;
    tss:hasField ex:headerless-time-field,
        ex:headerless-value-field .

ex:headerless-time-field
    a tss:ContextField ;
    tss:columnName "time" ;
    tss:columnIndex 0 ;
    tss:datatype xsd:dateTime .

ex:headerless-value-field
    a tss:ValueField ;
    tss:columnName "value" ;
    tss:columnIndex 1 ;
    tss:datatype xsd:double .
```

The data file, relative to the metadata document:

```
2024-05-01T00:00:00Z,12.5
2024-05-01T00:15:00Z,12.7
```
</div>

## Parquet ## {#parquet}

The data file of a `tss:ParquetSource` is an [Apache Parquet](https://parquet.apache.org/docs/file-format/) file, read by its column names: a field's column is the column whose name is its `tss:columnName`. `tss:delimiter`, `tss:encoding` and `tss:header` have no effect on it. The values keep their Parquet types in the file. To check a value against the field's datatype, the value is written in the lexical form that the table below gives for the Parquet type of its column, and the field's `tss:datatype` MUST be one of the datatypes the table allows for that type. A Parquet NULL is an [=Empty Value=]. A column of a Parquet type that is not in the table is not supported by this specification.

A Parquet file ends with a footer that indexes its row groups, so rows cannot be appended to it in place. A growing `tss:ParquetSource` is rewritten as a whole when readings are added, and consumers read it again as a whole: it cannot state `tss:rangeRequests true` ([[#range-opt-in]]). A producer that wants consumers to fetch only the new readings uses a CSV source, or an Arrow source in the IPC streaming format, instead.

<table class="data">
<thead>
<tr><th>Parquet type</th><th>Lexical form</th><th>Allowed <code>tss:datatype</code></th></tr>
</thead>
<tbody>
<tr><td><code>BOOLEAN</code></td><td><code>true</code> or <code>false</code></td><td><code>xsd:boolean</code></td></tr>
<tr><td><code>INT32</code>, <code>INT64</code>, <code>INT(8, 16, 32 or 64, signed)</code></td><td>decimal digits, with a <code>-</code> sign when negative</td><td><code>xsd:integer</code>, or the XSD integer type whose range holds the values, such as <code>xsd:int</code> or <code>xsd:long</code></td></tr>
<tr><td><code>INT(8, 16, 32 or 64, unsigned)</code></td><td>decimal digits</td><td><code>xsd:nonNegativeInteger</code>, or an unsigned XSD type such as <code>xsd:unsignedInt</code></td></tr>
<tr><td><code>FLOAT</code></td><td>the XSD lexical form of the value, with <code>INF</code>, <code>-INF</code> and <code>NaN</code> for the special values</td><td><code>xsd:float</code></td></tr>
<tr><td><code>DOUBLE</code></td><td>the same</td><td><code>xsd:double</code></td></tr>
<tr><td><code>DECIMAL(precision, scale)</code></td><td>decimal digits with a <code>.</code> and exactly <em>scale</em> fraction digits</td><td><code>xsd:decimal</code></td></tr>
<tr><td><code>STRING</code>, <code>ENUM</code></td><td>the text</td><td><code>xsd:string</code>, or any other datatype that reads a text</td></tr>
<tr><td><code>DATE</code></td><td><code>YYYY-MM-DD</code></td><td><code>xsd:date</code></td></tr>
<tr><td><code>TIMESTAMP(isAdjustedToUTC = true)</code>, in milli-, micro- or nanoseconds</td><td><code>YYYY-MM-DDThh:mm:ss[.fraction]Z</code>, in UTC, with a fraction only when the value has one</td><td><code>xsd:dateTime</code> or <code>xsd:dateTimeStamp</code></td></tr>
<tr><td><code>TIMESTAMP(isAdjustedToUTC = false)</code></td><td><code>YYYY-MM-DDThh:mm:ss[.fraction]</code>, without timezone, so it cannot be the column of the time field ([[#times]])</td><td><code>xsd:dateTime</code></td></tr>
<tr><td><code>TIME</code></td><td><code>hh:mm:ss[.fraction]</code></td><td><code>xsd:time</code></td></tr>
</tbody>
</table>

<div class="example" id="example-parquet-source">
The class and the data file of a Parquet source:

```turtle
ex:parquet-source
    a tss:ParquetSource ;
    tss:immutable true ;
    tss:dataFile <https://example.org/data.parquet> .
```
</div>

## Arrow ## {#arrow}

The data file of a `tss:ArrowSource` is written in one of the two IPC formats of Apache Arrow [[ARROW-IPC]]:

- the [IPC streaming format](https://arrow.apache.org/docs/format/Columnar.html#ipc-streaming-format): a schema message, followed by dictionary batches and record batches, and optionally an end-of-stream marker. A stream can be appended to in place, so it is the format of a growing source that states `tss:rangeRequests true` ([[#range-opt-in]]). Its media type is `application/vnd.apache.arrow.stream`, and its file extension is usually `.arrows`.
- the [IPC file format](https://arrow.apache.org/docs/format/Columnar.html#ipc-file-format): the same messages between the magic string `ARROW1` at the start and a footer at the end that indexes the record batches. Every append rewrites the footer, so a growing source in the file format is rewritten as a whole, and cannot state `tss:rangeRequests true`. Its media type is `application/vnd.apache.arrow.file`, and its file extension is usually `.arrow`.

The data file of an Arrow source that states `tss:rangeRequests true` MUST be in the IPC streaming format. The data file of any other Arrow source MAY be in either format. A consumer tells them apart by the first bytes of the file, not by its extension or media type: a file in the IPC file format starts with the 6 bytes `ARROW1`, a stream starts with the continuation marker `0xFFFFFFFF`. A server SHOULD serve the data file with the media type of its format.

The data file is read by its column names, as a Parquet file is: a field's column is the column whose name is its `tss:columnName`. `tss:delimiter`, `tss:encoding` and `tss:header` have no effect on it. The values keep their Arrow types in the file, and a dictionary-encoded column is read as the values it encodes. To check a value against the field's datatype, the value is written in the lexical form that the table below gives for the Arrow type of its column, and the field's `tss:datatype` MUST be one of the datatypes the table allows for that type. An Arrow null is an [=Empty Value=]. A column of an Arrow type that is not in the table is not supported by this specification.

<table class="data">
<thead>
<tr><th>Arrow type</th><th>Lexical form</th><th>Allowed <code>tss:datatype</code></th></tr>
</thead>
<tbody>
<tr><td><code>Bool</code></td><td><code>true</code> or <code>false</code></td><td><code>xsd:boolean</code></td></tr>
<tr><td><code>Int</code> (8, 16, 32 or 64 bits, signed)</td><td>decimal digits, with a <code>-</code> sign when negative</td><td><code>xsd:integer</code>, or the XSD integer type whose range holds the values, such as <code>xsd:int</code> or <code>xsd:long</code></td></tr>
<tr><td><code>Int</code> (8, 16, 32 or 64 bits, unsigned)</td><td>decimal digits</td><td><code>xsd:nonNegativeInteger</code>, or an unsigned XSD type such as <code>xsd:unsignedInt</code></td></tr>
<tr><td><code>FloatingPoint</code> (half or single precision)</td><td>the XSD lexical form of the value, with <code>INF</code>, <code>-INF</code> and <code>NaN</code> for the special values</td><td><code>xsd:float</code></td></tr>
<tr><td><code>FloatingPoint</code> (double precision)</td><td>the same</td><td><code>xsd:double</code></td></tr>
<tr><td><code>Decimal</code>(precision, scale)</td><td>decimal digits with a <code>.</code> and exactly <em>scale</em> fraction digits</td><td><code>xsd:decimal</code></td></tr>
<tr><td><code>Utf8</code>, <code>LargeUtf8</code>, <code>Utf8View</code></td><td>the text</td><td><code>xsd:string</code>, or any other datatype that reads a text</td></tr>
<tr><td><code>Date</code> (in days or in milliseconds)</td><td><code>YYYY-MM-DD</code></td><td><code>xsd:date</code></td></tr>
<tr><td><code>Timestamp</code> with a timezone, in seconds, milli-, micro- or nanoseconds</td><td><code>YYYY-MM-DDThh:mm:ss[.fraction]Z</code>, in UTC, with a fraction only when the value has one</td><td><code>xsd:dateTime</code> or <code>xsd:dateTimeStamp</code></td></tr>
<tr><td><code>Timestamp</code> without a timezone</td><td><code>YYYY-MM-DDThh:mm:ss[.fraction]</code>, without timezone, so it cannot be the column of the time field ([[#times]])</td><td><code>xsd:dateTime</code></td></tr>
<tr><td><code>Time</code></td><td><code>hh:mm:ss[.fraction]</code></td><td><code>xsd:time</code></td></tr>
</tbody>
</table>

<div class="example" id="example-arrow-source">
The class and the data file of two Arrow sources: an immutable one in the IPC file format, and a growing one in the IPC streaming format, to which record batches are still appended, and which consumers follow with Range requests ([[#appending-data-files]]).

```turtle
ex:arrow-source
    a tss:ArrowSource ;
    tss:immutable true ;
    tss:dataFile <https://example.org/data.arrow> .

ex:arrow-stream-source
    a tss:ArrowSource ;
    tss:rangeRequests true ;
    tss:dataFile <https://example.org/live.arrows> .
```
</div>

## Time grid ## {#time-grid}

An external source MAY give the time of each row as a step on a [=Time Grid=], instead of in a column of its own. It states the grid with `tss:timeBase`, the time of step 0, and `tss:timeStep`, the time between two steps, and MUST state both or neither. An embedded source MUST NOT state a time grid: every point states its own time.

- `tss:timeBase` is a time of the source ([[#times]]). It anchors the grid and is not a bound of the [=Time Window=]: it stays the same when `tss:from` or `tss:until` change, so no row's time moves.
- `tss:timeStep` is an `xsd:duration` [[XMLSCHEMA11-2]] of more than zero, such as `"PT15M"` or `"P1M"`.

Step n is at `tss:timeBase` + n × `tss:timeStep`: each part of the step (years, months, days, hours, minutes and seconds) is multiplied by n, and the result is added to `tss:timeBase` as XML Schema adds a duration to a date and time [[XMLSCHEMA11-2]].

Note: With years or months, the steps are calendar steps, not equal lengths of time: a month has 28 to 31 days, and a day past the end of a month becomes its last day (2021-01-31 + P1M is 2021-02-28).

A row's time is the time of its step: that is the row's value for the time field. The data file has no column for the time field, so the time field MUST NOT state a `tss:columnName` or a `tss:columnIndex`. The step of a row is given in one of two ways:

- **With a step field.** A column of the data file holds the step of each row. The source names the field of that column, the [=Step Field=], with one `tss:stepField`, as `tss:timeField` names the time field. The step field MUST be one of the source's own fields (`tss:hasField`), and MUST NOT be its time field. Its `tss:datatype` MUST be `xsd:integer`. Every row MUST have a step: a whole number of 0 or more. No two rows may be at the same step. Rows may be left out, and they may be in any order.
- **Without a step field.** The step of a row is its position in the data file: the first row, after the header line of a CSV file, is at step 0, the next at step 1, and so on. In a Parquet or Arrow file, the rows are counted across its row groups or record batches, in the order of the file. Every step up to the last row then has a row: a step without readings is a row with an [=Empty Value=] for every value field.

A step field is useful when the data has gaps; data that has been cleaned up to one row per step does not need one, and leaves out the overhead of a step column. A source without a time grid MUST NOT state a `tss:stepField`.

<div class="example" id="example-time-grid">
The readings of <a href="#example-embedded-source">the embedded example</a> in a CSV file, on a time grid of 15 minutes from 08:00. The column `step` holds the step of each row; the time field has no column.

```turtle
ex:grid-source
    a tss:CSVSource ;
    tss:immutable true ;
    tss:from "2024-05-12T08:00:00Z"^^xsd:dateTime ;
    tss:until "2024-05-13T00:00:00Z"^^xsd:dateTime ;
    tss:dataFile <https://example.org/grid.csv> ;
    tss:timeBase "2024-05-12T08:00:00Z"^^xsd:dateTime ;
    tss:timeStep "PT15M"^^xsd:duration ;
    tss:timeField ex:grid-time-field ;
    tss:stepField ex:grid-step-field ;
    tss:key ex:grid-time-field ;
    tss:hasField ex:grid-time-field,
        ex:grid-step-field,
        ex:grid-value-field .

ex:grid-time-field
    a tss:ContextField ;
    tss:datatype xsd:dateTime .

ex:grid-step-field
    a tss:ContextField ;
    tss:columnName "step" ;
    tss:datatype xsd:integer .

ex:grid-value-field
    a tss:ValueField ;
    tss:columnName "value" ;
    tss:datatype xsd:double .
```

The data file:

```
step,value
0,4810.5
1,4722.4
2,4751.9
5,4719.3
7,4783.6
```

Steps 3, 4 and 6 have no row. Step 5 is at 2024-05-12T08:00:00Z + 5 × PT15M, which is 2024-05-12T09:15:00Z.
</div>

<div class="example" id="example-time-grid-no-step">
The first three readings, which have no gaps, on the same time grid without a step field. The source states no `tss:stepField` and has no step column: each row is at the step of its position.

```turtle
ex:position-source
    a tss:CSVSource ;
    tss:immutable true ;
    tss:dataFile <https://example.org/position.csv> ;
    tss:timeBase "2024-05-12T08:00:00Z"^^xsd:dateTime ;
    tss:timeStep "PT15M"^^xsd:duration ;
    tss:timeField ex:position-time-field ;
    tss:hasField ex:position-time-field,
        ex:position-value-field .

ex:position-time-field
    a tss:ContextField ;
    tss:datatype xsd:dateTime .

ex:position-value-field
    a tss:ValueField ;
    tss:columnName "value" ;
    tss:datatype xsd:double .
```

The data file:

```
value
4810.5
4722.4
4751.9
```

The rows are at steps 0, 1 and 2: 08:00, 08:15 and 08:30.
</div>

## Value encoding ## {#value-encoding}

A field of an external source MAY state with `tss:valueEncoding` that the values in the data file are not the readings themselves. A field without a `tss:valueEncoding` states its readings: each value is its own [=Decoded Value=]. A field has at most one `tss:valueEncoding`, and the time field has none. The fields of an embedded source MUST NOT state `tss:valueEncoding` or `tss:baseline`: every point states the readings themselves.

This specification defines one value encoding, `tss:DeltaFromBaseline`: each value is the difference from the field's `tss:baseline`, and its decoded value is `tss:baseline` + the value. The sum is computed exactly on the decimal values of the two literals, and then read as the field's `tss:datatype`. The differences may be negative.

- A field with `tss:DeltaFromBaseline` MUST state exactly one `tss:baseline`: a number (`xsd:integer`, `xsd:decimal`, `xsd:double` or `xsd:float`) with the field's `tss:datatype`.
- A field without `tss:DeltaFromBaseline` MUST NOT state a `tss:baseline`.

`tss:minimum`, `tss:maximum` and the [=Stored Summary=] of the field describe the decoded values, not the differences, and so do the points of the field ([[#expanding]]).

<div class="example" id="example-delta-from-baseline">
The first two readings of <a href="#example-embedded-source">the embedded example</a> in a CSV file whose value column states the difference from a baseline of 4750.

```turtle
ex:delta-source
    a tss:CSVSource ;
    tss:immutable true ;
    tss:dataFile <https://example.org/delta.csv> ;
    tss:timeField ex:delta-time-field ;
    tss:key ex:delta-time-field ;
    tss:hasField ex:delta-time-field,
        ex:delta-value-field .

ex:delta-time-field
    a tss:ContextField ;
    tss:columnName "time" ;
    tss:datatype xsd:dateTime .

ex:delta-value-field
    a tss:ValueField ;
    tss:columnName "value" ;
    tss:datatype xsd:double ;
    tss:valueEncoding tss:DeltaFromBaseline ;
    tss:baseline "4750"^^xsd:double .
```

The data file:

```
time,value
2024-05-12T08:00:00Z,60.5
2024-05-12T08:15:00Z,-27.6
```

Their decoded values are 4750 + 60.5 = 4810.5 and 4750 + (-27.6) = 4722.4.
</div>

# Stored summaries # {#summaries}

A field of an immutable source MAY have one `tss:summary`: a node that holds numbers computed from the values of the field, such as how many values there are and their mean. A consumer can use them without reading the data. Every attribute of a summary is optional, and a summary may be empty.

A field of a [=Growing Source=] MUST NOT have a `tss:summary`: its numbers would be outdated by the next [=Write=], and the description of a growing source does not change ([[#change]]). A writer adds the summaries when it ends the source ([[#ending]]).

The numbers of a summary cover the rows in the [=Time Window=] of the source: from its `tss:from` (included) until its `tss:until` (not included), with an open side when the source leaves it open. A summary has no window of its own. They are computed from the [=Decoded Values=] of the field ([[#value-encoding]]).

A summary states with `tss:missingValuePolicy` which values its numbers leave out. This specification defines one such policy, `tss:ExcludeEmptyValues`: an [=Empty Value=] is not a value, so the numbers leave it out. A text of only spaces is a value.

The attributes of a summary:

<table class="data">
<thead>
<tr><th>Property</th><th>Value</th><th>Meaning</th></tr>
</thead>
<tbody>
<tr><td><code>tss:missingValuePolicy</code></td><td>IRI</td><td>which values the numbers leave out, such as <code>tss:ExcludeEmptyValues</code></td></tr>
<tr><td><code>tss:count</code></td><td><code>xsd:integer</code>, 0 or more</td><td>how many rows in the window have a value for the field; for a value field, how many points it has in the window</td></tr>
<tr><td><code>tss:missing</code></td><td><code>xsd:integer</code>, 0 or more</td><td>how many rows in the window have no value for the field; a time without a row is not counted</td></tr>
<tr><td><code>tss:firstTime</code>, <code>tss:lastTime</code></td><td>a time ([[#times]])</td><td>the time of the first and of the last row that has a value for the field</td></tr>
<tr><td><code>tss:observedMinimum</code>, <code>tss:observedMaximum</code></td><td>number</td><td>the smallest and the largest value</td></tr>
<tr><td><code>tss:mean</code></td><td>number</td><td>the unweighted arithmetic mean of the values</td></tr>
<tr><td><code>tss:median</code></td><td>number</td><td>the middle value, or the mean of the two middle values when their number is even</td></tr>
<tr><td><code>tss:generatedAtTime</code></td><td>a time ([[#times]])</td><td>when the numbers were computed</td></tr>
</tbody>
</table>

Only a numeric field ([[#datatypes]]) has `tss:observedMinimum`, `tss:observedMaximum`, `tss:mean` and `tss:median`. They include every value in the window, also values outside the expected range of the field: the observed minimum and maximum do not replace `tss:minimum` and `tss:maximum` ([[#meaning]]).

<div class="example" id="example-summary">
The summary of the value field of <a href="#example-embedded-source">the embedded example</a>, which the writer adds when it ends the source after its fifth row ([[#ending]]):

```turtle
ex:value-field
    tss:summary [
        tss:missingValuePolicy tss:ExcludeEmptyValues ;
        tss:count "5"^^xsd:integer ;
        tss:missing "0"^^xsd:integer ;
        tss:firstTime "2024-05-12T08:00:00Z"^^xsd:dateTime ;
        tss:lastTime "2024-05-12T09:45:00Z"^^xsd:dateTime ;
        tss:observedMinimum "4719.3"^^xsd:double ;
        tss:observedMaximum "4810.5"^^xsd:double ;
        tss:mean "4757.54"^^xsd:double ;
        tss:median "4751.9"^^xsd:double ;
        tss:generatedAtTime "2024-05-12T09:46:10Z"^^xsd:dateTime
    ] .
```
</div>

<div class="example" id="example-summary-missing">
The summary of the humidity field of <a href="#example-multi-sensor">the multi-sensor example</a>. One of the three rows has no humidity: it is counted in `tss:missing`, and the numbers leave it out.

```turtle
ex:humidity-field
    tss:summary [
        tss:missingValuePolicy tss:ExcludeEmptyValues ;
        tss:count "2"^^xsd:integer ;
        tss:missing "1"^^xsd:integer ;
        tss:firstTime "2024-05-12T08:00:00Z"^^xsd:dateTime ;
        tss:lastTime "2024-05-12T08:20:00Z"^^xsd:dateTime ;
        tss:observedMinimum "59.5"^^xsd:double ;
        tss:observedMaximum "61.0"^^xsd:double ;
        tss:mean "60.25"^^xsd:double ;
        tss:median "60.25"^^xsd:double ;
        tss:generatedAtTime "2024-05-12T09:00:00Z"^^xsd:dateTime
    ] .
```
</div>

A summary is computed once, from the final rows of the source. Its `tss:generatedAtTime` says when it was computed, and a consumer can compute the same numbers from the data to check it.

# Expanding a source into points # {#expanding}

A source of any class can be expanded into its points as RDF, in the shape of the points of a `tss:EmbeddedRDFSource` ([[#rdf-points]]). The expansion of a source is its metadata document, without `tss:points`, to which it adds the points of the source and what is needed to read them without this specification. To expand a source:

1. Read the rows: from the data file as [[#data-files]] says, or from the metadata as [[#points]] says.
2. Keep the rows whose time lies in the [=Time Window=]. A row without a time is an error.
3. For each row, and for each value field that has a value in it, make one point. The point is the RDF point itself for a `tss:EmbeddedRDFSource`, the IRI that `tss:aboutUrl` makes ([[#point-iris]]) when the value field states one, and a new blank node otherwise. Add:
    - the link from the source: the source `tss:hasPoint` the point;
    - the link to its value field: the point `tss:field` the field, also when the source has one value field;
    - the time: `tss:time`, with the time of the row as a literal with the time field's `tss:datatype`. On a [=Time Grid=], that is the time of the row's step;
    - the reading: `tss:value`, with the [=Decoded Value=] as a literal with the field's `tss:datatype`, or with its `tss:language` for `rdf:langString`. A value without a value encoding keeps its text as written;
    - for each describing field that describes the value field and has a value in the row, its field property with that value.
4. A value that is not in the lexical form of its datatype is an error.
5. For each describing field of the source that states no `tss:property`, add its default property: the field `tss:property` the default property ([[#point-values]]).

A document with several sources is expanded source by source: the points of a source only link to that source and its fields.

Note: The expansion keeps every value of a value field and of a describing field. The values of the other context fields, such as the step field or an identifier, are not stated on the points, though an identifier may be part of the IRI of a point ([[#point-iris]]).

<div class="example" id="example-expanded-csv">
The first row of <a href="#example-csv-source">the CSV example</a>, expanded:

```turtle
ex:csv-source tss:hasPoint [
    tss:field ex:csv-value-field ;
    tss:time "2024-05-01T00:00:00Z"^^xsd:dateTime ;
    tss:value "12.5"^^xsd:double
] .
```
</div>

## Alignment with SOSA and SAREF (informative) ## {#alignment}

*This section is informative.* This specification does not write its points with another vocabulary. The TSS vocabulary is aligned with SOSA [[VOCAB-SSN]], SAREF [[SAREF]] and QUDT instead, so that a reasoner can derive their observations and measurements from the expanded points when they are needed. The alignment is part of the TSS vocabulary:

<table class="data">
<thead>
<tr><th>TSS</th><th>Axiom</th><th>SOSA and QUDT</th><th>SAREF</th></tr>
</thead>
<tbody>
<tr><td><code>tss:Point</code></td><td><code>rdfs:subClassOf</code></td><td><code>sosa:Observation</code></td><td><code>saref:Measurement</code></td></tr>
<tr><td><code>tss:time</code></td><td><code>rdfs:subPropertyOf</code></td><td><code>sosa:resultTime</code></td><td><code>saref:hasTimestamp</code></td></tr>
<tr><td><code>tss:value</code></td><td><code>rdfs:subPropertyOf</code></td><td><code>sosa:hasSimpleResult</code></td><td><code>saref:hasValue</code></td></tr>
<tr><td><code>tss:field</code> followed by <code>tss:observedProperty</code></td><td><code>owl:propertyChainAxiom</code> of</td><td><code>sosa:observedProperty</code></td><td><code>saref:relatesToProperty</code></td></tr>
<tr><td><code>tss:field</code> followed by <code>tss:sensor</code></td><td><code>owl:propertyChainAxiom</code> of</td><td><code>sosa:madeBySensor</code></td><td><code>saref:measurementMadeBy</code></td></tr>
<tr><td><code>tss:field</code> followed by <code>tss:featureOfInterest</code></td><td><code>owl:propertyChainAxiom</code> of</td><td><code>sosa:hasFeatureOfInterest</code></td><td><code>saref:isMeasurementOf</code></td></tr>
<tr><td><code>tss:field</code> followed by <code>tss:unit</code></td><td><code>owl:propertyChainAxiom</code> of</td><td><code>qudt:hasUnit</code></td><td><code>saref:isMeasuredIn</code></td></tr>
</tbody>
</table>

Every point states its reading with `tss:value` ([[#point-values]]), so every reading is a result in SOSA and a value in SAREF. The observed property, sensor, feature of interest and unit are stated once, on the field, and reach each point through `tss:field`, which every expanded point states ([[#expanding]]). Property chains are part of OWL 2 RL [[OWL2-PROFILES]], so a rule reasoner can derive them. From <a href="#example-expanded-point">the expanded point of the embedded example</a>, it derives:

<div class="example" id="example-derived-observation">
```turtle
@prefix sosa: <http://www.w3.org/ns/sosa/> .
@prefix saref: <https://saref.etsi.org/core/> .
@prefix qudt: <http://qudt.org/schema/qudt/> .

[] a sosa:Observation, saref:Measurement ;
    sosa:resultTime "2024-05-12T08:00:00Z"^^xsd:dateTime ;
    sosa:hasSimpleResult "4810.5"^^xsd:double ;
    sosa:observedProperty quantitykind:ElectricConductivity ;
    sosa:madeBySensor ex:sensor-1 ;
    sosa:hasFeatureOfInterest ex:feature-1 ;
    qudt:hasUnit unit:MicroS-PER-CentiM .
```

and the same with SAREF terms.
</div>

The values of describing fields are not aligned: they say something about a reading, and are not results of their own.

Note: The alignment does not give a point a property of a domain vocabulary, such as `ex:temperature` for the readings of the temperature field of <a href="#example-multi-sensor">the multi-sensor example</a>. OWL cannot derive one: the values of `tss:value` are literals, and a property chain in OWL 2 only follows object properties. A rule language can, such as N3 or a SPARQL `CONSTRUCT` query. In N3: `{ ?point tss:field ex:temperature-field ; tss:value ?value } => { ?point ex:temperature ?value } .`

# Adding rows to a growing source # {#updating}

A [=Growing Source=] is append-only: rows are added, and an existing row or point is never changed or removed. Its rows only grow, but by default the bytes that hold them need not: a publisher MAY write its metadata document or data file anew on every change, for example to compress, sort or re-serialise it, as long as it holds the same rows and the new ones. A consumer then reads the whole document or data file again ([[#following-full]]).

## Opting in to Range requests ## {#range-opt-in}

A source that states `tss:rangeRequests true` promises more: the bytes that hold its rows only grow, so a consumer can fetch only the new bytes with HTTP Range requests ([[#range-requests]]), instead of reading the whole document or data file again on every change. While the source is growing, a representation that a server returned, without content coding, MUST then be a byte prefix of every later representation of the same resource in the same media type, as long as the resource is served at that IRI. This applies to:

- the metadata document of a `tss:EmbeddedRDFSource`, an RDF Message Log ([[#rdf-points]]);
- the data file of a `tss:CSVSource` ([[#appending-data-files]]);
- the data file of a `tss:ArrowSource`, which MUST then be in the IPC streaming format ([[#arrow]]).

Only these sources MAY state `tss:rangeRequests true`. The data file of a Parquet source, or of an Arrow source in the IPC file format, is rewritten on every append, and the `tss:points` literal of a `tss:EmbeddedJSONSource` is rewritten on every [=Write=]. Like the rest of the description of a growing source, `tss:rangeRequests` does not change while the source grows ([[#change]]). Its server SHOULD answer Range requests for the resource [[RFC9110]].

The promise is about the bytes as they are stored and served. A server MAY still compress a response with an HTTP content coding, as byte ranges then count the bytes without content coding ([[#range-requests]]). The resource itself MUST NOT be compressed as a whole, such as in a `.csv.gz` file: compressing the file again after an append rewrites earlier bytes, so a consumer would have to process the whole file again. A producer that has to rewrite earlier bytes, for example to compress, compact or correct the data, does not state `tss:rangeRequests true`, or publishes the result as a new source, under a new IRI.

## Embedded sources ## {#updating-embedded}

A [=Write=] that adds one row to a growing [=Embedded Source=]:

1. Checks the new row: every value is in the lexical form of its field's datatype, no other row has the same time (the [=Key=]), and its time lies in the [=Time Window=] of the source.
2. Adds the new row, and leaves everything else as it is. In a `tss:EmbeddedRDFSource`, it appends one RDF Message to the metadata document: one statement that links the source with `tss:hasPoint` to a new point for each value field that has a value in the row ([[#rdf-points]]), followed by a message delimiter. In a `tss:EmbeddedJSONSource`, it writes a new `tss:points`: the existing rows, unchanged and in the same order, followed by the new one as a JSON object whose values are JSON strings.
3. Keeps the change only when the source still meets this specification.

A Write that adds several rows at once does the same for each of them; in a `tss:EmbeddedRDFSource`, each row is a message of its own. A Write changes nothing else: not the time window, and not the description of the source or its fields ([[#change]]).

<div class="example" id="example-write">
A new row for <a href="#example-embedded-source">the embedded example</a>, appended to its RDF Message Log. It holds one point, as the source has one value field:

```turtle
ex:source tss:hasPoint [
    tss:time "2024-05-12T10:00:00Z"^^xsd:dateTime ;
    tss:value "4766.2"^^xsd:double
] .
MESSAGE
```

The new row lies in the window, which stays as it is. In <a href="#example-json-points">the JSON rows</a>, the same row is the object `{ "time": "2024-05-12T10:00:00Z", "value": "4766.2" }`, added at the end of the array in `tss:points`.
</div>

## Data files ## {#appending-data-files}

Rows are added to the data file of a growing [=External Source=], and no row is changed or removed. How a producer writes to the file is outside this specification. By default, it MAY write the whole file anew, in any format of its class. A file that a consumer reads MUST hold complete rows only: a CSV file ends with a line break, and an Arrow stream ends with a complete encapsulated message.

When the source states `tss:rangeRequests true` ([[#range-opt-in]]), the producer appends to the file in place, and the file MUST meet these rules after every append, so a consumer can read the new rows on their own ([[#range-requests]]):

- **CSV:** a writer appends complete records only, and ends every record with a line break. The header line and the earlier records MUST NOT change. A record that is not followed by a line break yet, is still being written.
- **Arrow:** the data file is in the IPC streaming format ([[#arrow]]). A writer appends complete encapsulated messages only: record batches, and dictionary batches, including delta dictionary batches, when a dictionary-encoded column needs new values. The schema message at the start of the stream MUST NOT change. A writer MUST NOT write the end-of-stream marker while the source is growing.

The time of a new row MUST lie in the [=Time Window=] of the source. On a [=Time Grid=], `tss:timeBase` and `tss:timeStep` do not change, so new rows continue the steps of the grid. Without a [=Step Field=], a new row is at the step of its position, so a writer appends a row for every step, with empty values for a step without readings.

## Ending a growing source ## {#ending}

A writer ends a growing source when no row will be added to it any more. It then:

1. Writes the last rows: the data file of an external source holds all its rows before the next step.
2. Adds `tss:immutable true` to the description of the source, and MAY add a [=Stored Summary=] to each field ([[#summaries]]). In the RDF Message Log of a `tss:EmbeddedRDFSource`, it appends one last message that holds these statements. In the metadata document of any other source, it adds them to the description.
3. In the IPC streaming format of an Arrow data file, MAY append the end-of-stream marker.

<div class="example" id="example-ending">
The last message of <a href="#example-embedded-source">the embedded example</a>, which ends the source and adds the summary of its value field (<a href="#example-summary">the summary example</a>):

```turtle
ex:source tss:immutable true .

ex:value-field tss:summary [
    tss:missingValuePolicy tss:ExcludeEmptyValues ;
    tss:count "5"^^xsd:integer ;
    # ... and the other numbers of the summary
] .
MESSAGE
```
</div>

The RDF Message Log of a growing source MUST NOT state `tss:immutable false`: the merge of all its messages would then state both `false` and `true`. Without it, the merge stays a conforming metadata document: it only gains `tss:immutable true` and the summaries. In a metadata document that is not a log, a writer replaces `tss:immutable false`, if stated, by `tss:immutable true`.

A consumer that reads `tss:immutable true` reads the rest of the rows once more, and then stops following the source ([[#following]]).

# Following a growing source # {#following}

A consumer that follows a [=Growing Source=] reads its rows, and later reads the rows that were added since. This section says how it finds out that rows were added over HTTP [[RFC9110]], how it reads them by default, and how it fetches only the new ones when the publisher enables Range requests. A source usually holds a whole time series ([[#intro]]). When a producer still spreads a series over several sources, finding them and moving on to the next one are left to a collection that contains them, such as the pages of a [Linked Data Event Stream](https://w3id.org/ldes/specification).

## Polling and caching ## {#polling}

A publisher of a growing source SHOULD send an `ETag` or a `Last-Modified` header with its metadata document and with its data file, and SHOULD answer conditional requests (`If-None-Match`, `If-Modified-Since`) with `304 Not Modified` when nothing changed. It SHOULD state with `Cache-Control: max-age` how long a consumer waits before it asks again, and a consumer SHOULD NOT ask more often. Once a source is immutable, its publisher SHOULD send `Cache-Control: immutable` with a long `max-age` for its metadata document and its data file.

A consumer of an [=External Source=] polls its metadata document and its data file each on its own. When the metadata document states `tss:immutable true`, all rows are in the data file ([[#ending]]): the consumer reads the data file once more, and stops.

## Reading the whole resource again ## {#following-full}

By default, a consumer that follows a growing source reads its metadata document, or its data file, again as a whole when it changed:

1. It asks for the resource with a conditional request ([[#polling]]). A `304 Not Modified` means that nothing was added.
2. On `200 OK`, it reads all rows of the new representation, and emits the rows it has not emitted before. The [=Key=] identifies a row: a row whose time it has emitted already is not emitted again.

A consumer MAY save work where the source keeps its rows in order. The existing objects of the `tss:points` of a `tss:EmbeddedJSONSource` do not change and stay in the same order ([[#updating-embedded]]): when the consumer has read *n* objects, it emits the objects after the first *n*, and only when the first *n* objects are not the ones it read, it compares all rows by their key.

This works for every source, but costs a full download and a full read on every change. A publisher that wants to spare its consumers that, states `tss:rangeRequests true` ([[#range-opt-in]]).

## Reading only the new bytes ## {#range-requests}

The RDF Message Log of a growing `tss:EmbeddedRDFSource`, and the data file of a growing `tss:CSVSource` or `tss:ArrowSource`, only grow when the source states `tss:rangeRequests true` ([[#range-opt-in]]). A consumer then does not download them again, but asks for the bytes after the ones it has read, with a Range request [[RFC9110]]. A consumer MUST NOT do so for a source that does not state `tss:rangeRequests true`: its earlier bytes may have changed. It reads them in units that a writer appends whole:

<table class="data">
<thead>
<tr><th>Source</th><th>Unit</th><th>A unit is complete when</th><th>State to keep</th></tr>
</thead>
<tbody>
<tr><td><code>tss:EmbeddedRDFSource</code></td><td>an RDF Message [[RDF-MESSAGES]]</td><td>a message delimiter follows it</td><td>the version label, the prefixes and the base IRI that are in force at the end of the last complete message</td></tr>
<tr><td><code>tss:CSVSource</code></td><td>a CSV record [[RFC4180]]</td><td>the line break that ends it follows; a line break inside a quoted cell does not end a record</td><td>the header line, and so the columns of the fields; on a [=Time Grid=] without a [=Step Field=], the number of rows read</td></tr>
<tr><td><code>tss:ArrowSource</code></td><td>an encapsulated message of the IPC streaming format [[ARROW-IPC]]</td><td>all its bytes are there: the continuation marker, the length of its metadata, the metadata, and a body of the length that the metadata states</td><td>the schema, and the dictionaries so far; on a [=Time Grid=] without a [=Step Field=], the number of rows read</td></tr>
</tbody>
</table>

A consumer that follows such a resource:

1. Reads the whole representation once, with a request without `Range`. It keeps the state of the table, and the offset *N*: the number of bytes up to the end of the last complete unit. A last unit that is not complete is not read yet.
2. Asks for the rest with `Range: bytes=N-`. It does not send `If-Range`: every append changes the validators of the resource, so `If-Range` would return the whole representation again. Byte offsets count the bytes of the representation without content coding, so the consumer SHOULD send `Accept-Encoding: identity` with a Range request.
3. Handles the answer:
    - `206 Partial Content` with a range that starts at *N*: it reads the new bytes with the state it kept, emits the rows of the complete units, and moves *N* to the end of the last complete unit.
    - `416 Range Not Satisfiable` with `Content-Range: bytes */L`: when *L* is *N*, nothing was added. When *L* is less than *N*, the resource shrank, and the consumer falls back (step 4).
    - `200 OK`: the server ignored the Range header and returned the whole representation. The consumer falls back (step 4) on that representation.
    - `410 Gone` or `404 Not Found`: the source is no longer served at this IRI; the consumer stops.
4. Falls back when the new bytes cannot be read as expected: when they do not start with a complete unit, do not parse with the state it kept, or when the resource shrank. It drops its state, and reads the whole representation again from byte 0, as in step 1. As in [[#following-full]], it does not emit again a row whose time it has emitted already.
5. Repeats from step 2, as often as the caching rules allow ([[#polling]]), until the source is immutable: it states `tss:immutable true`, or, for an Arrow stream, the end-of-stream marker follows the last complete unit.

A consumer MAY check that the bytes it read before did not change, by asking for `Range: bytes=(N-k)-` and comparing the first *k* bytes with the last *k* bytes it read. When they differ, it falls back (step 4). A server and a consumer MAY use an open-ended live range [[RFC8673]] to receive the new bytes as they are appended, without polling.

# RDF Message Stream Profile # {#message-profile}

RDF Time Series Sources is an RDF Message Stream Profile [[RDF-MESSAGES]]: it says which messages a stream of TSS readings holds, how these messages depend on each other, and where an RDF stream processing engine finds the focus nodes and the timestamp of each message. Two kinds of streams follow this profile:

- the RDF Message Log of a growing `tss:EmbeddedRDFSource` ([[#rdf-points]]);
- the expansion of a source of any class ([[#expanding]]), when it is written as an RDF Message Stream or Log: the description of the source and its fields first, then one message per row in the [=Time Window=], and an ending message when the source is immutable. A CSV, Parquet or Arrow source can so be streamed as RDF messages.

Note: The section on profiles in RDF Messages is still being written. This profile follows the direction of the RDF Stream Processing Community Group: a profile says what its messages look like, where their timestamps are, and how messages relate to each other.

## Messages ## {#message-kinds}

A stream that follows this profile holds three kinds of messages:

<table class="data">
<thead>
<tr><th>Message</th><th>What it holds</th><th>Focus nodes</th><th>Timestamp</th></tr>
</thead>
<tbody>
<tr><td>description message</td><td>the description of one or more sources and their fields, as in a metadata document, without points; in an expansion, also the default field properties of its describing fields ([[#expanding]], step 5)</td><td>each source: the subject of an <code>rdf:type</code> that is a source class</td><td>none</td></tr>
<tr><td>row message</td><td>one [=Row=] of one source: the source links with <code>tss:hasPoint</code> to one point for each value field that has a value in the row ([[#rdf-points]])</td><td>each point: an object of <code>tss:hasPoint</code></td><td>the time of the row</td></tr>
<tr><td>ending message</td><td><code>tss:immutable true</code> for a source, and optionally the [=Stored Summaries=] of its fields ([[#ending]])</td><td>the source</td><td>none</td></tr>
</tbody>
</table>

- The messages of a source are not independent: a row message is read with the description of its source, as RDF Messages allows a profile to say. The description message of a source MUST come before its row messages, and its ending message after them. A consumer that starts reading a stream after its description message needs the description from elsewhere, such as from the start of the RDF Message Log.
- A row message holds exactly one row of exactly one source, and nothing else. The points of a row all have the same time.
- The timestamp of a row message is the time of its row: on each of its points, the object of `tss:time`, so that an engine finds it without the description message. `tss:time` is a subproperty of `sosa:resultTime` ([[#alignment]]), the property that RDF Messages suggests to assume as the timestamp of SOSA observations.
- The order of the messages is the order in which the rows were written, which need not be the order of their times. An engine orders rows by their timestamp.
- Every message ends with a message delimiter, as [[#rdf-points]] requires for the log of a growing source.

## Announcing the profile ## {#announcing-profile}

The profile is identified by `tss:MessageStreamProfile`: a `prof:Profile` [[DX-PROF]] of RDF Messages. A publisher MAY announce that a stream or log follows the profile:

- in the stream itself, in its first description message, by stating that the document or stream conforms to it: `<> dcterms:conformsTo tss:MessageStreamProfile`;
- over HTTP, with a link to the profile [[RFC6906]]: `Link: <https://w3id.org/tss#MessageStreamProfile>; rel="profile"`.

<div class="example" id="example-message-stream">
<a href="#example-csv-source">The CSV example</a>, expanded and written as an RDF Message Log that follows the profile: a description message, then one row message per row.

```turtle
VERSION "1.2-messages"
PREFIX tss: <https://w3id.org/tss#>
PREFIX dcterms: <http://purl.org/dc/terms/>
PREFIX rdfs: <http://www.w3.org/2000/01/rdf-schema#>
PREFIX xsd: <http://www.w3.org/2001/XMLSchema#>
PREFIX ex: <https://example.org/ex#>

<> dcterms:conformsTo tss:MessageStreamProfile .

ex:csv-source a tss:CSVSource ;
    tss:timeField ex:csv-time-field ;
    tss:hasField ex:csv-time-field, ex:csv-value-field .
# ... and the rest of the description of the source and its fields
MESSAGE
ex:csv-source tss:hasPoint [
    tss:field ex:csv-value-field ;
    tss:time "2024-05-01T00:00:00Z"^^xsd:dateTime ;
    tss:value "12.5"^^xsd:double
] .
MESSAGE
ex:csv-source tss:hasPoint [
    tss:field ex:csv-value-field ;
    tss:time "2024-05-01T00:15:00Z"^^xsd:dateTime ;
    tss:value "12.7"^^xsd:double
] .
MESSAGE
```

An engine takes the timestamp of each row message from `tss:time`, and its focus node from `tss:hasPoint`.
</div>


# Vocabulary overview # {#vocabulary}

*This section is informative.* All terms are in the namespace `https://w3id.org/tss#`.

<table class="data">
<thead>
<tr><th>Term</th><th>Kind</th><th>Used on</th><th>Section</th></tr>
</thead>
<tbody>
<tr><td><code>tss:Source</code></td><td>class</td><td>-</td><td>[[#the-source]]</td></tr>
<tr><td><code>tss:EmbeddedJSONSource</code>, <code>tss:EmbeddedRDFSource</code>, <code>tss:ParquetSource</code>, <code>tss:CSVSource</code>, <code>tss:ArrowSource</code></td><td>class, subclass of <code>tss:Source</code></td><td>-</td><td>[[#storage]]</td></tr>
<tr><td><code>tss:Field</code></td><td>class</td><td>-</td><td>[[#fields]]</td></tr>
<tr><td><code>tss:ValueField</code>, <code>tss:ContextField</code></td><td>class, subclass of <code>tss:Field</code></td><td>-</td><td>[[#field-kinds]]</td></tr>
<tr><td><code>tss:Point</code></td><td>class</td><td>-</td><td>[[#rdf-points]], [[#expanding]]</td></tr>
<tr><td><code>tss:Summary</code></td><td>class</td><td>-</td><td>[[#summaries]]</td></tr>
<tr><td><code>tss:MissingValuePolicy</code>, <code>tss:ValueEncoding</code></td><td>class</td><td>-</td><td>[[#summaries]], [[#value-encoding]]</td></tr>
<tr><td><code>tss:ExcludeEmptyValues</code></td><td>individual</td><td>value of <code>tss:missingValuePolicy</code></td><td>[[#summaries]]</td></tr>
<tr><td><code>tss:DeltaFromBaseline</code></td><td>individual</td><td>value of <code>tss:valueEncoding</code></td><td>[[#value-encoding]]</td></tr>
<tr><td><code>tss:MessageStreamProfile</code></td><td>individual, a <code>prof:Profile</code></td><td>object of <code>dcterms:conformsTo</code></td><td>[[#message-profile]]</td></tr>
<tr><td><code>tss:immutable</code></td><td>property</td><td>source</td><td>[[#change]]</td></tr>
<tr><td><code>tss:rangeRequests</code></td><td>property</td><td><code>tss:EmbeddedRDFSource</code>, <code>tss:CSVSource</code>, <code>tss:ArrowSource</code></td><td>[[#range-opt-in]]</td></tr>
<tr><td><code>tss:hasField</code>, <code>tss:timeField</code>, <code>tss:key</code></td><td>property</td><td>source</td><td>[[#time-and-key]]</td></tr>
<tr><td><code>tss:from</code>, <code>tss:until</code></td><td>property</td><td>source</td><td>[[#window]]</td></tr>
<tr><td><code>tss:hasPoint</code></td><td>property</td><td><code>tss:EmbeddedRDFSource</code>, and any source when expanded</td><td>[[#rdf-points]], [[#expanding]]</td></tr>
<tr><td><code>tss:field</code></td><td>property</td><td>point</td><td>[[#rdf-points]], [[#expanding]]</td></tr>
<tr><td><code>tss:time</code>, <code>tss:value</code></td><td>property</td><td>point</td><td>[[#point-values]], [[#alignment]]</td></tr>
<tr><td><code>tss:points</code></td><td>property</td><td><code>tss:EmbeddedJSONSource</code></td><td>[[#json-points]]</td></tr>
<tr><td><code>tss:dataFile</code></td><td>property</td><td>external source</td><td>[[#data-files]]</td></tr>
<tr><td><code>tss:delimiter</code>, <code>tss:header</code>, <code>tss:encoding</code></td><td>property</td><td><code>tss:CSVSource</code></td><td>[[#csv]]</td></tr>
<tr><td><code>tss:timeBase</code>, <code>tss:timeStep</code>, <code>tss:stepField</code></td><td>property</td><td>external source</td><td>[[#time-grid]]</td></tr>
<tr><td><code>tss:producedBy</code>, <code>tss:sourceDescription</code></td><td>property</td><td>source</td><td>[[#provenance]]</td></tr>
<tr><td><code>tss:columnName</code>, <code>tss:columnIndex</code>, <code>tss:datatype</code>, <code>tss:language</code>, <code>tss:minimum</code>, <code>tss:maximum</code></td><td>property</td><td>field</td><td>[[#fields]], [[#datatypes]]</td></tr>
<tr><td><code>tss:valueEncoding</code>, <code>tss:baseline</code></td><td>property</td><td>field of an external source</td><td>[[#value-encoding]]</td></tr>
<tr><td><code>tss:property</code></td><td>property</td><td>describing field</td><td>[[#point-values]]</td></tr>
<tr><td><code>tss:aboutUrl</code></td><td>property</td><td>value field</td><td>[[#point-iris]]</td></tr>
<tr><td><code>tss:observedProperty</code>, <code>tss:unit</code>, <code>tss:sensor</code>, <code>tss:featureOfInterest</code></td><td>property</td><td>field</td><td>[[#meaning]]</td></tr>
<tr><td><code>tss:describesField</code>, <code>tss:fieldDescription</code></td><td>property</td><td>field</td><td>[[#describing]]</td></tr>
<tr><td><code>tss:summary</code></td><td>property</td><td>field</td><td>[[#summaries]]</td></tr>
<tr><td><code>tss:missingValuePolicy</code>, <code>tss:count</code>, <code>tss:missing</code>, <code>tss:firstTime</code>, <code>tss:lastTime</code>, <code>tss:observedMinimum</code>, <code>tss:observedMaximum</code>, <code>tss:mean</code>, <code>tss:median</code>, <code>tss:generatedAtTime</code></td><td>property</td><td>summary</td><td>[[#summaries]]</td></tr>
</tbody>
</table>

The vocabulary reuses `rdfs:label`, `rdfs:comment`, the XML Schema datatypes [[XMLSCHEMA11-2]] and the datatype `rdf:JSON` [[JSON-LD11]]. It is aligned with SOSA, SAREF and QUDT ([[#alignment]]), and with CSVW [[TABULAR-METADATA]] through `skos:closeMatch`.

<h2 class="no-num" id="conformance">Conformance</h2>

The key words "MUST", "MUST NOT", "SHOULD" and "MAY" in this document are to be interpreted as described in BCP 14 [[!RFC2119]] [[!RFC8174]] when, and only when, they appear in all capitals.

Everything in this specification is normative, except the sections marked as informative, the examples, the notes and the issues.

This specification defines four conformance classes:

: <dfn export>Conforming metadata document</dfn>
:: An RDF document that describes at least one source and meets every requirement in this specification on a metadata document, a source, a field, the field property of a describing field, embedded rows and points, and a stored summary. For an external source, this includes the requirements on the properties that describe the data file, its [=Time Grid=] and its value encoding, but not on the contents of the data file.
: <dfn export>Conforming data</dfn>
:: The rows of a source, embedded or in its data file, that meet the requirements on rows and points: the time of every row (on a [=Time Grid=], its step), the key, the lexical form of the values, the [=Time Window=], and the IRIs of the points. The rows of an embedded source are part of its metadata document; the rows of a data file are not.
: <dfn export>Conforming consumer</dfn>
:: Software that reads a conforming metadata document and its data as this specification says: it selects the rules for each source by its class, reads the rows as [[#points]] or [[#data-files]] says, finds the [=Field Property=] of each describing field as [[#point-values]] says, reads the times as [[#times]] says, decodes the values of a field with a value encoding ([[#value-encoding]]), applies the [=Time Window=] and the [=Empty Value=] rules, and reports an error where this specification calls something an error. When it follows a [=Growing Source=], it does so as [[#following]] says. A <dfn export>conforming expander</dfn> is a conforming consumer that also expands a source into points as [[#expanding]] says.
: <dfn export>Conforming writer</dfn>
:: Software that changes the rows of an [=Embedded Source=] only by the [=Write=] of [[#updating-embedded]], that only appends to the data file of a [=Growing Source=] as [[#appending-data-files]] says, that ends a growing source as [[#ending]] says, and that leaves a conforming metadata document conforming after every change.
